.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
serves this host's YANG models (RFC 7950) on cblink's YANG family,
YANG-CBOR (RFC 9254) over TLS 1.3, DTLS on SCTP, or a local socket: it
reports the jails, the network, the storage, the services and the rest
of this host and changes them for clients such as the arcana web
console and
.Xr arcanactl 8 .
It is the only privileged part of arcana.
.Pp
What it reads of the host's daemons and files, each when asked, are
actions of their modules by cblink's YANG family:
.Xr sshd 8 ,
of the host or of a jail (arcana-sshd's
.Ic read ) ;
.Xr poudriere 8 ,
of the host or of a jail (arcana-builder's
.Ic read
and
.Ic port-options ) ;
the cores dumped (arcana-crashes); the system's own files and settings
(arcana-system); the NFS server, iSCSI's target and HAST
(arcana-netstore); Suricata's rules, its rule sources, an instance's
answers and its profiling (arcana-suricata); OpenSMTPD (arcana-smtpd)
and UDPspeeder's relays (arcana-speeder), of the host or of a jail;
SSLproxy's SSL inspection, its CA's key and certificate (arcana-sslproxy),
of the host or of a jail; the
admin's monitors (arcana-probe's
.Ic read
with
.Ic monitors ) .
A Telegram message is arcana-notify's
.Ic notify .
.Pp
Beside them it serves
.Ql arcana-stream ,
a cblink family of its own whose commands open streams with
credit-based flow control: a snapshot sent
.Pq Ic zfs-send
and one received
.Pq Ic zfs-receive
by
.Xr zfs 8 ,
a capture put for a Suricata instance to replay
.Pq Ic suricata-pcap-put
and a file of its pcap-log got
.Pq Ic suricata-pcap-get ;
its bodies are arcana-stream's structures.
A replica, received from a stream or made by
.Ic replicate
on the host, takes from the stream what it holds of the data and of its
properties but these, which are the receiving side's
.Po
.Xr zfs-receive 8
.Fl u ,
.Fl x
and
.Fl o
.Pc :
it is not mounted, and
.Cm canmount
is
.Cm noauto ,
so it is not mounted at the next boot either;
.Cm mountpoint ,
.Cm setuid ,
.Cm exec ,
.Cm devices ,
.Cm jailed ,
.Cm overlay ,
.Cm sharenfs ,
.Cm sharesmb
and
.Cm keylocation
are inherited from the dataset it is received under (or, for one that
is not inherited, its default), as if the stream had none of them
.Pq Xr zfsprops 7 .
They are the console's alone, as every request is, and refused unless
NACM permits them.
.Pp
Running jails are read with
.Xr jail_getv 3
and their processes with
.Xr libprocstat 3 .
Configured jails are read with
.Ql jail -f file -e
for
.Pa /etc/jail.conf
and every
.Pa /etc/jail.conf.d/*.conf
that
.Pa /etc/jail.conf
does not include, the files
.Pa /etc/rc.d/jail
would use, so variables, includes and shared statements mean what they
mean to
.Xr jail 8 .
.Pp
Starting, stopping and restarting run
.Xr jail 8
with
.Fl c ,
.Fl r
or
.Fl rc
and the file that defines the jail, so the jail's
.Va exec.*
hooks, mounts and interfaces are handled as usual.
Killing removes the jail with
.Xr jail_remove 2 .
Addresses change in the running jail with
.Xr jail_setv 3
and, when the jail has an
.Va interface ,
an alias added or removed on it; in the configuration they change in
place with libjailconf, as
.Xr jailconf 8
does.
The previous file is kept as
.Pa file.arcana.bak ,
and if
.Xr jail 8
does not read the new file as intended it is put back.
.Pp
Beyond jails it reports and changes the host itself, always through the
system's own programs.
Services are the executable scripts of
.Pa /etc/rc.d
and
.Pa /usr/local/etc/rc.d :
whether they are enabled is what
.Ql sysrc -A
says, read from the
.Xr rc.conf 5
files themselves, or by it where reading alone does not tell (a command
in them, a computed value),
whether the enabled ones run from
.Ql service name status ;
they are started, stopped, restarted and reloaded with
.Xr service 8
(arcana-services' actions, its
.Ic read
the one service's state); enabled and disabled, and their
.Xr rc.conf 5
variables set, by a commit of arcana-services
.Pq Sx CONFIGURATION ,
or by a job with
.Xr sysrc 8 .
The routing table, the nexthops, the active connections and the open
sockets are what
.Xr netstat 1
and
.Xr sockstat 1
print with
.Fl -libxo Cm json ,
one run per address family.
Usage figures are the CPU share from
.Va kern.cp_time
between two requests, memory from the
.Va vm.stats.vm
counters, swap from
.Xr swapinfo 8
and the ZFS pools from
.Xr zpool 8 :
arcana-host's
.Ic host ,
with what
.Xr uname 3
and the sysctls say of the host, read when asked and nothing else with
it.
The certificates OpenSSL trusts, of the host or of a jail, are its
.Ic read-certificates
action: each of
.Pa /etc/ssl/certs
and
.Pa /etc/ssl/untrusted
read with OpenSSL, which no read of the host waits for.
Packages that the repository has newer versions of are listed with
.Ql pkg version -vRl '<'
and upgraded with
.Ql pkg upgrade -y ,
on the host or, with
.Ql pkg -j ,
in a running jail.
What runs is arcana-processes' list, read with
.Va kern.proc.proc
twice for the CPU shares between the looks; one process's files and
threads its
.Ic read
action, with
.Xr libprocstat 3 .
A log is read by arcana-log's
.Ic read
action, of the log directory or a jail's own, the files there with
it.
How busy the host has been is arcana-history's
.Ic read ,
the samples after a time with how they are taken; how it samples and
its limits are arcana-history's configuration, a commit of it written
to its history file
.Pq Fl 1 .
What happened on the host is arcana-events'
.Ic read ,
the events after a number, and its
.Ic event
notification, each as it happens.
Packets are followed through the firewall by arcana-firewall's
.Ic trace ,
the kernel's
.Ql ipfw:::rule-matched
probe told to
.Xr dtrace 1 ;
what the host does with a packet and its reply, nothing sent, by
arcana-troubleshoot's
.Ic path-check ,
one round of
.Xr traceroute 8
by its
.Ic traceroute ,
and packets taken with
.Xr tcpdump 1
by its
.Ic capture
(without an interface, the sources there are).
The network tables are arcana-network's read actions, the host's or a
running vnet jail's, read from the host: the routes through
.Xr netlink 4
(2000 at most, those with a text, read on from an offset), the nexthops
and nexthop groups as
.Xr netstat 1
prints them, the connections and sockets from the kernel's control
block lists, the interfaces by
.Xr getifaddrs 3
with their
.Xr carp 4
hosts, the bridges and
.Xr geneve 4
tunnels by
.Xr ifconfig 8 ,
the neighbours, IGMP's and MLD's state by
.Xr ifmcstat 8 ,
the static routes
.Xr rc.conf 5
keeps, strongSwan's IPsec by
.Xr swanctl 8 ,
a vnet jail's firewall, and the route to an address; a leaf named as
the program names the value.
The routes, connections and sockets also come in parts as a stream of
the agent's own family, arcana-parts, a part sent only when its reader
has room for it.
A jail's address is arcana-jails' jail's
.Ic address
action (arcana-network's).
.Pp
The kept rules of the firewall
.Pq Fl J
are arcana-firewall's
.Pa kept/text ,
written by a commit, read by ipfw
.Fl n
first, loading nothing.
Installing or updating the agent changes no firewall variable and
writes no firewall file.
The host's first commit moves its boot to Arcana's own script beside
the kept file,
.Pa ipfw.sh
(0700, the file loaded by ipfw
.Fl q ) ,
which
.Va firewall_script
names in Arcana's
.Xr rc.conf 5
file; the host's own firewall script and
.Pa /etc/rc.firewall
are never edited.
That commit is a migration: refused, nothing written, unless the rules
it would load are those the kernel's set 0 has now, as ipfw writes
them back; arcana-firewall's
.Ic from-rc
makes them of what the host boots now.
A confirmed commit whose time is up puts every file it changed back.
.Pp
RFC 8519's access control lists
.Pq Li ietf-access-control-list
are compiled to
.Xr ipfw 8 :
an ACL a band of rule numbers of its own from 61000, its ACEs one rule
each, ended by a
.Ic return ;
an interface's ingress ACLs a
.Ic call
from rules 60001 to 60999
.Pq Ic in recv ,
its egress ones
.Pq Ic out xmit ,
entered by a
.Ic call
at rule 8; rule 60000 skips to 65000.
Each rule's comment says its ACL and ACE.
The whole is loaded into set 28, disabled, and swapped with set 29 at
once; it is kept as
.Pa acls.cbor
beside the agent's files and applied again when the agent starts.
A change is refused, nothing loaded, where a rule of the admin's is at
rule 8 or 60000 to 64999, or in set 28 or 29, and where an ACE matches
what ipfw cannot (an ICMP code, IPv6's hop limit, TCP's options and the
like).
An ACL with an Ethernet match decides on the IP pass alone: its
Ethernet ACE tags the frame on the layer-2 pass
.Pq Ic layer2 mac ... tag
and the ACE's rule looks for the tag
.Pq Ic not layer2 tagged ;
it is refused attached on an egress, and unless
.Va net.link.ether.ipfw
is 1, which the agent never sets.
RFC 9899's defined sets
.Pq Li ietf-acl-enh
are compiled into the ACEs that name them: a prefix set an
.Ic addr
table
.Pq Ic src-ip table(T) , dst-ip table(T) ,
a port set a port list, or a
.Ic number
table
.Pq Ic lookup dst-port T
past 30 ranges, a protocol set an or-block of
.Ic proto ,
an ICMP type set
.Ic icmptypes
or
.Ic icmp6types ,
an alias its destination prefixes' table, its protocols and its ports.
The tables are named
.Li acl-0-*
and
.Li acl-1-* ,
the ACLs' as sets 28 and 29 are: an edit fills the generation not in
use before its rules are loaded into set 28, the swap makes both live,
and the old generation is destroyed after it; no table is ever swapped.
A rule of the admin's naming one refuses a change, as does
.Va net.inet.ip.fw.tables_sets
1.
RFC 9899's matches are what
.Xr ipfw 8
reads of them: the fragment types IPv4's
.Ic frag
flags
.Pq Ic df , mf , offset ,
IPv6's
.Ic ext6hdr frag
and
.Ic frag offset ,
the TCP flags' bitmask
.Ic tcpflags ,
each operator's alternatives an or-block
.Pq Ic "{ frag mf or frag offset }" ,
an extension header
.Ic ext6hdr .
What
.Xr ipfw 8
cannot read is refused, whatever the operator: IPv6's last fragment
(it reads no M flag), its first fragment unless atomic fragments are
dropped
.Pq Va net.inet6.ip6.fw.permit_single_frag6 No 0 and Va deny_unknown_exthdrs No 1, the admin's ,
the flags
.Ic tcpflags
does not name (ECE, CWR, AE, the reserved bits), and the mobility, HIP
and shim6 headers.
RFC 9899's actions: a rate-limit is a
.Xr dummynet 4
pipe of the ACE's own,
.Ic pipe P config bw
the bytes per second times eight in bit/s, the ACE's rule
.Ic pipe P
for
.Ic allow
(a rate of 0
.Ic deny ) ;
the pipes are numbered from the ACE's rule, 41000 to 44999 and 51000 to
54999 as the tables' generations, configured before the swap and the
old ones deleted after it.
It is refused where dummynet is not loaded, which the agent never
does, and where
.Va net.inet.ip.fw.one_pass
is 0.
A log-action is the rule's
.Ic log logdst ,
its log-id
.Ic syslog
(none given),
.Ic bpf
or
.Ic rtsock ;
a counter-action a
.Ic count
rule a name before the ACE's rules.
The operational datastore has the ACLs the kernel has whole, the
prefix sets as their tables hold them, and the named counters
.Pq Li arcana-acl
as their rules count.
.Pp
What the kernel's PF_KEY tells of the host's SAs is told as RFC 9061's
IKE-less notifications
.Pq Li ietf-i2nsf-ikeless ,
to those who subscribed: a socket of the agent's own,
.Dv SADB_REGISTER Ns ed ,
hears
.Dv SADB_ACQUIRE ,
an outbound packet meeting a policy with no SA
.Pq Li sadb-acquire , the policy and its selector ,
and
.Dv SADB_EXPIRE ,
an SA's soft or hard lifetime reached
.Pq Li sadb-expire , what it carried and how long it has been there ;
every ten seconds the SAs' sequence counters are read and one without
ESN an eighth from its end is told once
.Pq Li sadb-seq-overflow .
An SA or a policy of the model's lists is named as the controller
named it; the rest, setkey's, an IKE daemon's, by the kernel's own:
an SA
.Li esp/ Ns Ar destination Ns Li /0x Ns Ar SPI ,
a policy
.Li spd/ Ns Ar id ;
the host's network only, not a vnet jail's.
There is no
.Li sadb-bad-spi ,
which PF_KEY has no message for
.Pq Li arcana-ikeless-deviations .
.Pp
The model's SAD and SPD lists
.Pq Li ipsec-ikeless
are the kernel's.
Running holds the controller's entries, kept in
.Pa ikeless.cbor
beside the agent's files (mode 0600: the keys are in it), each put in
by PF_KEY as it is configured: an SA by
.Dv SADB_ADD ,
read back by
.Dv SADB_GET ,
a policy by
.Dv SADB_X_SPDADD ,
or
.Dv SADB_X_SPDUPDATE
in place of one of the controller's of the same selector.
An edit changes what differs alone, policies out, SAs out, SAs in,
policies in; a step refused undoes the steps before it.
The operational datastore has every ESP SA and every policy of the
host's network, the controller's by their names, the rest by the
kernel's and never changed: an entry that would take one's SPI and
destination, or its selector and direction, is refused
.Pq Li in-use .
An SA is from its local end to its remote one, and a selector's local
prefix is its packets' source, as RFC 9061's Appendix B has them; the
controller's policies are global, of the priorities from 0x7fa20000 in
the list's order.
The SAs' soft and hard lifetimes are the kernel's
.Dv SADB_EXT_LIFETIME_SOFT
and
.Dv SADB_EXT_LIFETIME_HARD
but packets and idle, which the agent reads with the counters: soft
told as
.Li sadb-expire ,
hard the SA taken out and told.
What the kernel's IPsec does not do is refused
.Pq Li operation-not-supported :
ESP in TCP, NAT-T's original addresses, a DF bit per SA, DSCP
mappings, PFP, port ranges, algorithms other than AES-CBC and
AES-GCM-16 with HMAC-SHA1 and HMAC-SHA2.
.Va net.key.preferred_oldsa
is set to 0 once an SA is put in.
At the start the controller's policies are put back where the kernel
has not got them; its SAs the kernel has lost leave running, for the
controller to send new ones (RFC 9061 section 4.2).
.Pp
An SD-WAN tunnel's SAs are keyed by the agent itself
.Pq Li arcana-ikeless :
a key pair
.Pq Li key-pairs/key-pair ,
X25519, kept as
.Pa sdwan-key- Ns Ar interface Ns Pa .pem
beside the agent's files (0600), made with its entry, made anew when
its generation changes (the one before kept, put back when the
generation is), its public half in the operational datastore, the
.Cm sdwan
read and the
.Li sdwan-key
event; a derived sad-entry names its pair, the far end's public half,
an epoch and its direction and is given no SPI and no keys: the agent
derives them by HKDF-SHA256 of the X25519 agreement, the same on both
ends, and keeps them in running.
An SA the same in all under another name is no change to the kernel,
so a rotation's next SAs become the tunnel's own without a packet
lost; a derived SA the kernel has already from the same derivation is
taken over as it is.
An entry with a
.Li jail
is of that vnet jail's network, by PF_KEY there; a jail's pair is
named
.Ar interface Ns @ Ns Ar jail .
A pair whose SAs an edit could not change is a fault
.Pq Li key-rotation
until an edit leaves it one SA each way.
.Pp
Every request has a trace: the library calls, system calls, files and
programs it used, with the output of every program, told to the peer
that asked as it runs (arcana-jobs'
.Ic steps
notification), a job's kept with it.
Changes are made one at a time; reading is not held up by them.
Jobs of pkg in jails are the exception: those at the head of the queue,
each in a jail of its own, run up to four at once, a jail's own one
after another, so that a download stalled in one jail holds up no
other; the next job of any other kind waits for all of them.
Such a job can be cancelled while it runs: its pkg is stopped.
A job keeps who asked for it: its
.Ic user ,
the request's NACM user (the console's user it acted for, or
.Dq console ) ,
in its list entry, its read and the event of its end; none for the
recovery session, whose
.Ic peer
says who it was.
.Pp
Every operation requires an authenticated peer.
On a local socket that is anyone who can open it: the socket is mode
0600, or 0660 with
.Fl g .
On the network it is a peer with a client certificate issued by
.Ar cafile ,
over TLS 1.3.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl A Ar cafile
Certificate authority for client certificates.
.It Fl C Ar certfile , Fl K Ar keyfile
This server's certificate and key.
The three files are read at startup, before the daemon detaches: one
that is missing, unreadable, not PEM, or a key that is not the
certificate's, is an error that names the file.
.It Fl D Ar confdir
Per-jail configuration files: where a commit of arcana-jails puts a
jail new, a file of its own,
.Ar name Ns .conf .
Default
.Pa /etc/jail.conf.d .
Its files, with the one of
.Fl f
and the templates, are arcana-jail-files'
.Ic read-files
action on the jails, read when asked: no read of the jails waits for
them.
.It Fl d
Log debugging messages.
.It Fl F
Stay in the foreground and log to standard error.
.It Fl f Ar jail.conf
Main configuration file.
With the files of
.Fl D ,
arcana-jails' running datastore: each jail's parameters as
.Xr jail 8
.Fl e
resolves them, which a commit writes in the jail's own block, in
place, every other line kept, read back by
.Xr jail 8
before it stands; and whether rc.d/jail starts it,
.Va jail_list
and
.Va jail_enable
in Arcana's rc.conf file.
Default
.Pa /etc/jail.conf .
.It Fl g Ar group
Make local sockets mode 0660 and owned by
.Ar group .
.It Fl j Ar jail
The
.Xr jail 8
program.
.It Fl l Ar uri
Listen on
.Ar uri ,
which may be given up to eight times:
.Ql unix:/path ,
.Ql tcp://address:port
or
.Ql sctp://address[,address ...]:port .
Network listeners need
.Fl A ,
.Fl C
and
.Fl K .
Default
.Ql unix:/var/run/arcana-agent.sock .
Beside every
.Ql tcp://
listener
.Nm
listens on SCTP at the same address and port, where the kernel has
SCTP.
It does not load the
.Xr sctp 4
module, and where it is missing does not try again by itself: it is
told to with arcana-agent's
.Ic retry
action of its
.Ic transport ,
as
.Xr arcanactl 8
.Cm transport retry
does.
On an SCTP association an event stream subscription (the NETCONF
stream: arcana-steer's
.Ic steer-changed ,
arcana-events' events, a tunnel's probes among them) is sent on an
SCTP stream of its own, ahead of what else the association carries: a
large read or dump on the same connection does not hold it up.
Over TCP the events wait behind what was sent before them.
The listeners, the connections open and each SCTP association's paths
are that container's state; its
.Ic listen
action listens on another address, closes one and keeps the change in
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
.It Fl p Ar pidfile
Default
.Pa /var/run/arcana-agent.pid .
.Xr rc 8
stops and restarts the agent by the pid in it, so the agent keeps it
its own: every few seconds, and before it restarts itself after an
update, a pidfile removed
.Pq Pa /var/run No cleaned under it ,
written over, or replaced by another file is written back with its pid,
a fault of type
.Ql pidfile
set and cleared.
One another arcana-agent holds is left alone, the fault saying so.
After an update, a restart
.Xr service 8
did not do (the daemon still runs 10 seconds on) is done by the
daemon's pid: a process of its own asks
.Xr rc 8
whether the service is enabled, stops the daemon, and starts the
service unless
.Xr rc 8
says it runs; not enabled, the program before is left running and the
log says so.
.It Fl R Ar rc.d dirs
The directories whose scripts are the host's services, colon separated.
Default
.Pa /etc/rc.d:/usr/local/etc/rc.d .
.It Fl s Ar service
The
.Xr service 8
program, which lists, queries and changes services.
.It Fl y Ar sysrc
The
.Xr sysrc 8
program, which reads and writes their
.Xr rc.conf 5
variables.
.It Fl U Ar program
The file an update replaces.
The default is the running program.
.It Fl B Ar dir
Where a tree is cloned and built, the build user's alone, mode 0700.
Default
.Pa /var/cache/arcana-build .
.It Fl -build-user Ar user
Whom git, make and ssh-keygen run as for a build when the daemon is
root.
Default
.Li _arcanabuild .
It must be a user nothing else is: not root, not
.Li nobody ,
with no process of its own running when a build starts and no home
of its own
.Po
.Pa /nonexistent ,
.Pa /var/empty
or the build directory
.Pc ;
a build is refused otherwise, and when there is no such user, with
the
.Xr pw 8
line that makes it.
.Nm
makes no user itself.
Named here, it is checked when the daemon is not root too.
.It Fl c Ar config.mk
This host's settings for a build, copied into the tree when the file
exists.
Default
