.Sh MAPPED LOGINS
arcana-stream's
.Cm terminal-as
opens a terminal on which
.Ql login -f -h Ar address Fl - Ar account
runs, so that a console user the host's admins mapped to an account
gets that account's login without typing its password.
The console user is the request's own
.Pq Xr cblink 3 Ns 's user, the name NACM judged :
there is no ticket or signature beside it, for whoever holds the
console holds every host anyway, by signed agent updates and the
.Pa authorized_keys
files it writes.
The operation is
.Ql nacm:default-deny-all :
it runs only for a user a rule permits it to: the group admin, and the
group operator by its rule-list's
.Ql terminal-as
rule, which the console synchronizes only to a host whose exec-default
is permit; with deny, an admin adds one by hand, in a rule-list of its
own.
Only the console, the socket's group
.Pq Fl g
or root on the unix socket may ask; a request for no user, or for the
console's own user but root's, is refused.
.Pp
The host decides by
.Fl -terminal-users ,
lines of
.Bd -literal -offset indent
scope account [+root] console-user ...
.Ed
.Pp
with
.Ar scope
.Ql host
or
.Ql jail: Ns Ar name ,
never one for the other, and
.Ql #
beginning a comment.
An account of uid 0, root or any other name for it, is allowed only by a
line that says
.Ql +root .
A word beginning with
.Ql +
is a flag, never a console user, and
.Ql +root
the only one; another refuses the file.
No job, system file, bundle or YANG node writes the file.
While a mapped session lasts its way is walked again every 2 seconds and
its lines read again whenever it changed; a session it no longer allows
is hung up.
The account is
.Ql [a-z_][a-z0-9_-]{0,31} .
.Pp
.Xr login 1
with
.Fl f
asks no password and never calls
.Xr pam_acct_mgmt 3 ;
on the host the agent makes those checks before it forks, in the
session's own thread after the stream is accepted: the account is
there, not locked, not expired, its password not past its change time,
its shell is not
.Xr nologin 8 ,
.Pa /var/run/nologin
(the class's
.Ql nologin )
is not there unless the class has
.Ql ignorenologin
or the account is uid 0,
and the class's times, hosts and ttys let it in.
.Xr login.access 5
and securetty are not consulted on this path: the file stands for them.
The session counts as established only once
.Xr utmpx 5
says that account logged in on its terminal; until then what is typed
waits, and another account, or none within 10 seconds, hangs it up.
In a jail the jail's
.Xr getent 1 ,
by
.Xr jexec 8 ,
proves the account there first: not locked, of uid 0 only by
.Ql +root ,
a shell that logs in; what is typed waits until login wrote anything.
That runs in the session's own thread after the stream is accepted, and
a refusal closes the stream; the jail's utmpx is not read, so a jail's
account is never said to be confirmed.
.Pp
Each open, refusal and end is logged to
.Ql LOG_AUTH
at
.Ql notice :
the console user, the account, the scope, the terminal and the address.
Accounts whose password is empty, which login lets anyone in as, are
one fault
.Ql empty-password
on
.Ql accounts
that says how many and never which; the names go to
.Ql LOG_AUTH
alone.
They are looked for at the start and every ten minutes.
.Sh ITS OWN SERVICE
A stop or restart of
.Nm arcana_agent
asked of the daemon itself, as a service action, is not run and waited
for as other services' are:
.Xr service 8
would wait for the daemon to stop while the daemon waits for it to end.
It is handed to a process of its own, the request is answered, and the
daemon is stopped by that process a moment later.
.Sh STOPPING
On
.Dv SIGTERM
or
.Dv SIGINT ,
as from
.Ql service arcana_agent stop ,
the daemon stops.
A change that is under way is not cut short: the daemon logs what it
is and which jobs wait behind it, starts nothing more, and stops when
the change has ended, reporting again every 30 seconds while it waits.
The jobs that had not started are lost.
A second signal stops it at once, and the log says what was cut short.
A program a change had started keeps running in that case.
.Sh INTERFACES
The network interfaces are read with
.Xr getifaddrs 3
and a few ioctls, as
.Xr ifconfig 8
reads them: flags, link state, MTU, hardware address, description, the
two ends of a tunnel, counters and addresses.
They are changed by jobs that run
.Xr ifconfig 8 :
an address added as an alias or taken off, an interface brought up or
down.
Static routes are added and deleted by jobs that run
.Xr route 8 .
The changes are live only.
.Pp
The interfaces are also the host's service attachment points, RFC
9408's network
.Li arcana-sap
over RFC 8345's, state alone: the host is its node, each interface,
the vnet jails' with them, a termination point, and each that a
service can be attached to a SAP of the SD-WAN: an Ethernet interface
.Pq Li phy , No or Li logical No when cloned ,
a
.Xr lagg 4 ,
a
.Xr vlan 4
on its parent, an
.Xr if_bridge 4 ;
not a lagg's port or a bridge's member, nor a tunnel.
An
.Xr epair 4
end's peer is its other end.
A SAP's status is its interface's
.Li oper-status ,
told on change with the interfaces.
.Pp
A job of these kinds may ask for safe mode.
The change is made, and the job waits, in the state
.Li unconfirmed ,
for the client to confirm it; reaching the daemon again is the point.
Without that within the wait
.Pq Fl w , 60 seconds
the daemon undoes the change, as it also does when it is stopped while
waiting, or when the job is cancelled while it waits
.Pq its Li cancel action :
an address is put back with the prefix length it had, an
interface brought back up or down, a route deleted or added again, a
sysctl set to the value it had and its line in
.Pa sysctl.conf
put back or taken out, a route deleted without its gateway added back as
the kernel listed it, a BIRD protocol enabled or disabled again, and
BIRD's configuration taken back by BIRD itself
.Pq Cm configure undo ,
which read it with a timer a little longer than the wait, and is told to
keep it
.Pq Cm configure confirm
when the job is confirmed, and a WireGuard interface synced back
.Pq Cm wg syncconf
from what
.Cm wg showconf
printed before, a text held in memory only and never written to the
trace, with its
.Xr wg-quick 8
file and the
.Pa rc.conf
variables as they were, and an IPsec connection's files put back before
.Xr swanctl 8
reads them again, its SAs ended first when the connection was new, and a
file of the system's written whole put back and its daemon told again,
and a tunnel, tap, tun or loopback interface destroyed made again as it
was read before, a bridge's member, flag, vlanfilter or static address
taken back by the inverse word, a geneve made destroyed again.
What has to be read before the change for that, and cannot be, fails
the job before the change is made.
.Pp
arcana-jobs'
.Li dry-run
rpc runs the changes submit would queue dry, at once and with no job:
every step a job would take is taken, but a program is run only when it
reads
.Pq Cm sysrc -n , service status , jail -e ,
and any other is a step with its arguments and no exit status; a file
written, an address taken off with an ioctl,
.Xr jail_remove 2
or the daemon's own restart is a step saying so, and is not done.
Jobs of the kinds jail-action, service-action, service-config, if-state,
if-address, route, sysctl, kmod and reboot can be run so; any other is
refused, not previewable.
Given a job waiting to be confirmed instead, it says what undoing it
would do now, the job held while it does and left as it was, neither
confirmed nor cancelled.
Its rights are submit's.
.Sh BUILDS
A client may ask for
.Nm
to be built from a git repository: the console does when
.Va agent_source
is set, and
.Xr arcanactl 8
with
.Cm agent-build .
The repository is cloned afresh, without history, with its submodules,
into the build directory, and
.Pa cblink
and
.Pa jailconf ,
the submodules,
.Pa lib/libvarconf
and
.Pa usr.sbin/arcana-agent
are built there with
.Xr make 1 .
When the daemon is root, git, make and ssh-keygen run as the build
user
.Pq Fl -build-user .
Root opens nothing in the build directory by name: the directory is
opened once, not through a link, and checked to be the build user's
and written by no one else; the program built and
.Pa id_ed25519.pub
are opened beneath it a name at a time, no link followed, checked to
be regular files of the build user's with one link, and read through
that descriptor; the program's SHA-256 is logged.
For a repository reached over SSH, the server's host key is never
taken on trust: until the client sends back the keys a person
confirmed, the reply carries the keys the server showed and their
fingerprints, and nothing is built.
Confirmed keys are kept in
.Pa known_hosts
in the build directory and checked strictly from then on.
git shows the server a key of this host's, made on first use as
.Pa id_ed25519
there, or the one the client names.
The program is sent back with its SHA-256 and the version it reports,
compressed with gzip, with the size it inflates to
.Pq Ic program-size ;
the SHA-256 is still the program's, and nothing is installed.
One build runs at a time.
.Pp
With
.Cm console-update
the whole tree is built, as the unprivileged user, and installed with
.Ql make install
as the daemon; the
.Nm arcana_console
service is restarted after the reply.
That is how a console updates itself, through the agent of its host.
.Sh UPDATES
A client may send a new
.Nm
by arcana-agent's
.Ic update
action: the console does, and
.Xr arcanactl 8
with
.Cm agent-update .
The program comes compressed with gzip
.Pq Xr zlib 3 ,
with the size it inflates to
.Pq Ic program-size ,
in a frame of 8 MiB with the rest of the request; one without
.Ic program-size
is refused.
The daemon inflates it first: to no more than that size, nor than
32 MiB, stopping a byte past it.
The console and arcanactl send an update only to an agent that has
.Li gzip-update
in arcana-host's agent features; an agent before has no features
there and is updated by hand.
The program, inflated, is checked against its signature by the
console's CA key (asked of every client but the local socket's) and
the SHA-256 sent with it, written beside
the installed one, and run with
.Fl V ;
one that does not run on this host is dropped.
It then takes the place of the installed program, which is kept with
the suffix
.Pa .old .
When asked to, the daemon has
.Xr service 8
restart
.Nm arcana_agent
after it has replied; jobs still queued are lost, one that is running
ends first.
.Sh RC.CONF VARIABLES
The service
.Nm arcana_agent
always listens on its local socket; network listeners are added to it.
.Bl -tag -width "arcana_agent_listen"
.It Va arcana_agent_enable
Run the agent.
.It Va arcana_agent_socket
The local socket.
Default
.Pa /var/run/arcana-agent.sock .
.It Va arcana_agent_group
A group whose members may use the socket
.Pq Fl g .
Default: none, root only.
.It Va arcana_agent_listen
Network listeners, separated by spaces, such as
.Ql tcp://0.0.0.0:4310 tcp://[::]:4310 .
Default: none.
.It Va arcana_agent_ca , arcana_agent_cert , arcana_agent_key
The files for
.Fl A ,
.Fl C
and
.Fl K ,
used when there are network listeners.
Default:
.Pa ca.pem ,
.Pa host.pem
and
.Pa host.key
in
.Pa /usr/local/etc/arcana .
.It Va arcana_agent_flags
More flags, added to those the variables above give.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Pa /var/run/arcana-agent.sock
.It Pa /var/run/arcana-agent.pid
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/arcana/feeds/
The threat feeds' tables, a file each.
.It Pa /usr/local/etc/arcana/terminal-users
Who may become which account by a mapped login.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Serve the console host over TLS and local tools over the default
socket:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# arcana-agent -A /usr/local/etc/arcana/ca.pem \e
    -C /usr/local/etc/arcana/host.pem \e
    -K /usr/local/etc/arcana/host.key \e
    -l unix:/var/run/arcana-agent.sock \e
    -l tcp://0.0.0.0:4310 -l 'tcp://[::]:4310'
.Ed
